  "stage": "homily-review",
  "summary": "The revised First Sunday of Advent homily passes a continuous reading for an adult parish assembly. In Luke 21:25\u201333 Jesus describes people fainting with fear, then tells his disciples why they can raise their heads when the signs begin. The Collect lets a hearer who has helped keep a quarrel alive ask to meet that same Lord, and Romans 13:8\u201314a gives a reason to go back to the friend who heard only one side. I checked that argument against the reviewed interpretation, the 1962 Missal pp. [1]\u20132, Challoner, Gregory\u2019s Homily 1 \u00a7\u00a72\u20135, Chrysostom\u2019s Homily 24 on Romans and Augustine on Psalm 84 \u00a7\u00a710, 12. Gregory\u2019s weary traveler still wants the road to continue, Chrysostom\u2019s runners increase their effort near the prize, and Jesus draws the inference about the nearness of the kingdom from the trees himself in Luke 21:31. The source note correctly assigns the imagined reconciliation to the homily. TeXcount gives 1,162 spoken words, or 10.1\u201311.1 minutes at the stated pace of 105\u2013115 words per minute. Delivery was assessed from the written speech, without a timed human performance."
